From 6f842234e384cfd7c1339cb2a417f45a249b20cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:33:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: remove the temporary per-frame timing diagnostics

Patches 0010-0026 added Time::getCurrentTime()-based instrumentation
(RENDERDBG.LOG/FLIPDBG.LOG/PUMPDBG.LOG/LOGICDBG.LOG) to find the 486DX2-50
+Mach64 performance campaign's dominant costs. Each block was explicitly
marked "TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC... to be reverted once measured".

The 2026-08-30/31 S3 ViRGE investigation used them to build a full
per-stage cost model on the LFB+16bpp path (see docs/PLAN.md's "Phase
0/2 result"), then found real-hardware A/B evidence that the
instrumentation itself has real cost: removing all 5 diagnostic
timing/logging blocks (kept every real code path -- the render loop,
blowupOntoScreenNative, SDL_UpdateWindowSurface/Rects, the audio pump
while loop, all untouched) recovered a real, repeatable ~0.4-0.5fps on
the 486DX2-66+S3 ViRGE rig (13.94/13.84fps with the diagnostics vs.
14.38/14.50fps without, across four otherwise-identical real-hardware
runs). A shipping build has no business paying per-frame
fopen/fprintf/fclose overhead for measurements already taken.

Follow-up bisection (also real-hardware verified, not committed as a
permanent diagnostic) found the remaining gap to the 15fps KPI target
is not a rendering-cost problem: real per-frame work (render+flip+
pump+rest-of-loop) totals only ~45.6ms against the 66.7ms/frame budget
for 15fps, so lockedFrameRate=15's own frame limiter is already
correctly sleeping ~21ms by design. The residual ~2-5ms shortfall looks
like ordinary SDL_Delay() overshoot on this DOS backend, not something
this patch addresses.
